% ============================================================================
% sun-layer — two named layers of the Sun and what each actually is
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% The Sun is not a single uniform ball — it is built of distinct layers, and
% two of the outermost ones each have a clean, one-fact name for what they
% are. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? sun_layer(photosphere, $D)
%       % the_visible_surface_of_the_sun
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `sun_layer(layer, description)` carrying the source citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a solar-layer term that is not one of these two. The query
% also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the layer it names:
%
%     ? sun_layer($L, the_suns_outer_atmosphere)
%       % corona
%
% The two layers, as a little truth table:
%
%     layer          description                    quote (verbatim)
%     -------------  -----------------------------  --------------------------------------
%     photosphere     the_visible_surface_of_the_sun   "The Photosphere – the visible surface of the Sun."
%     corona          the_suns_outer_atmosphere        "The Corona – the Sun's outer atmosphere."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from NASA's "Layers of the Sun" blog post
% (The Sun Spot). WebFetch-verified twice — the second pass pulled the full
% surrounding paragraph for every layer named on the page, confirming
% `photosphere` and `corona` are the only two whose own sentence states a
% single clean fact. `trust authoritative` — NASA is a primary .gov source,
% the same tier the sibling `celestial-objects.adj`/`comet-part.adj`/
% `space-rock-stage.adj`/`lunar-eclipse-type.adj` (the other libraries in
% this directory) already use for their NASA citations.
%
% Only two rows are shipped, an intentionally smaller table than most
% siblings in this directory — the honest-abstention discipline applies to
% table SIZE as much as to individual queries. Honest abstention on
% `chromosphere`: a real solar layer the SAME page names ("The Chromosphere
% – an irregular layer above the photosphere where the temperature rises
% from 6000°C to about 20,000°C.") — but that single sentence bundles the
% layer's position TOGETHER WITH its temperature range as one grammatically
% unified relative clause, rather than stating one clean fact the way
% `photosphere` and `corona` do. `core`, `radiative zone`, and `convection
% zone` are real layers the same page also names, but each of their
% sentences bundles location together with process/structure details in
% the same way — none of them is a row here either. So a recall on any of
% these ABSTAINS rather than inventing a single-fact description the
% source's own sentence doesn't cleanly state. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table sun_layer {
    columns layer, description

    row (photosphere, the_visible_surface_of_the_sun)
    row (corona, the_suns_outer_atmosphere)

    source "The Photosphere – the visible surface of the Sun."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/the-sun-spot/2023/09/26/layers-of-the-sun/"
    trust authoritative
}
